## Context

The author previewed the next public build of the JETP observatory and found
three defects on the Documents page, to fix before the site is republished.
Author's decisions (2026-09-25), quoted, not to be relitigated:

1. **One row per document, history folded.** `data/documents.json` has one row
   per retrieval attempt (`row_key` `<id>:<n>`); 34 of 294 documents show 2-3
   rows (e.g. blocked, blocked, collected; or two HTTP 404 attempts for
   `sen-offgrid-mini-grid-2025`). "The document list must show ONE row per
   document: its best attempt (a collected attempt if any, the latest
   collected if several; otherwise the latest attempt), with every attempt
   listed in a small folded line, e.g. '▸ 2 attempts: 404 on 13 Sep 08:46,
   404 on 13 Sep 09:14'. Nothing hidden, the retrievals data table
   unchanged." Filters and counts count documents consistently.
2. **Dead-link row.** `sen-offgrid-mini-grid-2025` was never collected (HTTP
   404 on both attempts, 2026-09-13), yet the page said "publisher link dead
   since 24 September 2026" (the first link check). "'Dead since' must use
   the earliest evidence: the earliest retrieval attempt of that URL that
   returned 404/410, or the link check's dead_since, whichever is earlier.
   And when a document was never collected, say so plainly: 'No copy: the
   file was already gone when we tried to collect it.'" Author-approved mock:

       Off-Grid Europe Mini-Grid 2025 (FR)   SEN  missing
         Publisher's page — offgrideurope.com  · publisher link dead (404) since 13 Sep 2026
         No copy: the file was already gone when we tried to collect it.
         ▸ 2 attempts: 404 on 13 Sep 08:46, 404 on 13 Sep 09:14

   Keep the unreachable-is-never-dead rule.
3. **Remove the per-row byte-identity notes** ("A Web Archive capture of a
   web page is not byte-identical to what we read." and the SHA-256
   sentences next to "Web Archive copy — …"): "a recurring false alarm".
   Explain once, on the Methods page, what the SHA-256 lets a reader check
   and why an archived web page differs from the bytes we read.

Served views stay in normal form (one file = one table, joins at read time):
the grouping and the dead-since join are made in `app.js`.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The Documents page shows one row per document id (294), its best
      attempt per the rule above, with a folded line listing every attempt
      when there are several; status/method/type facets and the count line
      count documents, on the best attempt.
- [ ] "Dead since" is the earliest of the link check's `dead_since` and the
      earliest 404/410 retrieval of that URL after its last successful one;
      a link the check found unreachable is never dead.
- [ ] A never-collected document whose attempts found the file gone says
      "No copy: the file was already gone when we tried to collect it."
- [ ] No per-row identity note beside a Web Archive copy; the Methods page
      explains the SHA-256 and the archived web page once.
- [ ] Render tests first for each behaviour; the dead-link positive control
      still fails when the dead-link code is broken.
- [ ] Browser recipe `tests/browser/jetp_observatory.py` green on the local
      preview and on the public bundle; `make check-fast`, `make lint`, and
      the full `make check` on padme green.
